  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-110
TITLE: External ADC Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with two external ADC IC chips. Each chip has one channel, and they are
responsible for reading the mA and kV feedback signals. Each chip uses a dedicated SPI bus and chip
select line.

The external ADC module acquires both channels using nonblocking SPI receive DMA transfers. Each
received frame is decoded and checked for valid device framing. DMA, framing, and acquisition timing
errors produce an invalid result and prevent that result from being used for feedback processing.

Completed paired samples are averaged before the result is published to the feedback and control
functions. The ADC chip-select signals remain inactive while the ADCs are idle.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-10
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-11
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-111
TITLE: External DAC communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with four external DAC IC chips. Each chip has one channel each and are
responsible for setting the grid voltage control signal, filament current control signal, high
voltage control signal, and emission current limit control signal. All DAC chips are identical make
and model, and the firmware communicates with all chips on a singular SPI bus, using independent
chip select signals for each chip. The external DAC module waits for any queued-up requests by the
rest of the firmware to write new DAC values. In case multiple DAC values are queued up simultaneous
or in quick succession, the DAC module processes them in FIFO (first in first out) fashion, waiting
for the SPI bus to be free until the next value is processed.

The DAC module utilizes the manufacturer's hardware abstraction library to write serial data to the
SPI bus. The writes to the SPI bus are non-block interrupt-based calls which allows the processor to
continue operating during the relatively longer SPI transmission (compared to processing speed). The
DAC module utilizes the SPI interrupt handler to set flags for handling the queued DAC requests
(indicating bus is free).

In addition to sending data over the SPI bus, the DAC module handles the digital chip select
signals. These signals are independent for each chip, so there are a total of 4 digital output lines
controlled by the DAC via the hardware abstraction library's provided GPIO control.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-112
TITLE: Input Debouncing
STATEMENT: >>>
The digital inputs are debounced by sampling the state of the input signals every time a processing
flag gets set. This processing flag is set every 1 msec. Once an input displays a toggled value, the
firmware begins a debounce countdown. During this debounce period, any bouncing signals are ignored.
After the debounce period is over, the firmware checks to see if the input truly did toggle or if it
was just noise. If the signal indicates a 'true' toggle, the firmware updates the changed input
states for processing as well as reporting to the main control application firmware.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-5
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-113
TITLE: Interlock Enable
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware monitors the interlock signals coming from the main control board. These signals can
independently, from the HVPS interface microcontroller, disable emission. However, for redundancy,
the firmware will also disable its own outputs to the HVPS to terminate emission if it detects that
the interlock signals are requesting to disable emission.

Additionally, the firmware will proceed to 'lock' its own emission output controls and disallow
emission until the main control board sends commands to unlock the controls. The unlock sequence
requires that the main control board send these commands while the interlocks are specifically in a
'disabled' state and then toggle the interlocks into an 'enable' state within a certain time period.
This is to ensure that the hardware signals are functioning as the firmware expects and are able to
disable emission if needed. This lock and unlock process is required for both the high voltage
interlock and grid interlock signals coming from the main control board.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-114
TITLE: HVPS Interface Status Flags
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware tracks various Boolean status flags. These status flags are represented in a single
bitmask with each bit representing one flag. This status is reported to the main control board. The
following flags are monitored and reported:

- HV interlock control enabled
- Grid interlock control enabled
- Filament ramp in progress
- High voltage ramp in progress
- Emission ongoing
- Configuration modification unlocked
- PID enabled
- Calibration grid interlock enabled
- Fast filament ramping enabled
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-115
TITLE: Output Power
STATEMENT: >>>
The output power is set by the main control board via commands to the firmware. The firmware
utilizes the power setpoint along with the high voltage output setpoint so that PID control of the
emission output targets the associated emission current (power divided by voltage).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-8
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-116
TITLE: High Voltage Output and Ramping
STATEMENT: >>>
When a target filament high voltage value is requested by the main control board, the firmware will
incrementally ramp up or down towards the final target value.

Unfortunately, the high voltage power supply is unregulated and has a relatively inaccurate control
translation (differential between the control signal and feedback signal is around +/-5% tolerance
which is too far for clinical performance) and as such the firmware must implement a closed loop to
adjust the control to arrive at a more accurate high voltage output.

When increasing voltage, the firmware increases the control output, with larger steps the further
away from the target value. Once the system starts to approach the target high voltage, the firmware
uses smaller increment steps. This is due to the time lag in the system where increases in the
control signal to the high voltage power supply take a short but indeterminate amount of time to
fully stabilize. Stabilization time depends on a variety of factors such as HVPS calibration/tuning,
as well as high voltage cable and source characteristics. The simplest way to account for all these
differences is to use smaller steps the closer to the target output to eliminate time lag issues.

For decreasing, once again due to both time lag issues, the decrements are generally small, but if
the target voltage is much lower, the firmware will drop the control signal to approximately 90% of
the target value (to account for the unregulated tolerance variances of the HVPS).

The increment/decrement steps are performed at fixed periodic intervals while the ramping process is
taking place. While the ramping process is taking place the firmware also sets the 'high voltage
ramping' status bit in the flag status value reported to the main control board.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-8
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-117
TITLE: Filament Output and Ramping
STATEMENT: >>>
When a target filament current value is requested by the main control board, the firmware will
incrementally ramp up or down towards the final target value. The decrement steps are large and
fixed. The increment steps are variable and are dependent on two factors. The first factor is the
present output value of the filament current. Higher present filament currents will result in
smaller steps than lower present filament currents. The second factor is whether fast ramping is
enabled or not. If fast ramping is enabled, the step size will be increased by a fixed multiple.

As the final target filament current may not be a multiple of the final step size, if the final step
under or overshoots the final target value, the ramp simply sets the filament current on this final
step to the intended target value.

The increment/decrement steps are performed at fixed periodic intervals while the ramping process is
taking place. While the ramping process is taking place the firmware also sets the 'filament
ramping' status bit in the flag status value reported to the main control board.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-6
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-17
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-118
TITLE: Grid Output
STATEMENT: >>>
The grid is currently just set to a fixed constant value. The main control board may change the grid
output value to another fixed value. The grid value is not ramped, it is directly set through the
external DAC interface.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-7
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-119
TITLE: Emission Limit Output
STATEMENT: >>>
The emission current limit signal is set to a fixed output value
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-9
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-120
TITLE: Emission Current Grid Control
STATEMENT: >>>
When grid control is enabled, the firmware calculates the difference between the requested emission
current and the measured feedback current. It adjusts the grid output in response to that error and
constrains the resulting command to the configured minimum and maximum grid outputs.

The grid-control calculation runs in the FreeRTOS control task. The resulting command is written
through the external DAC interface.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-121
TITLE: DMA Grid Clock
STATEMENT: >>>
The HVPS requires a toggling grid clock signal to allow emission. The firmware uses a timer update
event and DMA to write alternating set and reset values to the grid-clock GPIO output. This produces
the grid-clock waveform without a timer interrupt handler.

Enabling the clock resets the output low, initializes the DMA transfer, and starts the timer.
Disabling the clock stops the timer and DMA transfer and leaves the output low.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-1
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-122
TITLE: External Feedback Control Task
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware creates a dedicated FreeRTOS task for external ADC result processing and feedback
control. The task waits for notification that an ADC acquisition has completed, accepts only valid
results, updates feedback values, processes the high-voltage ramp, and calculates output-control
commands.

Shared status and control state are protected while accessed by the task and interrupt context.
DAC writes are performed outside the protected state-processing region so external feedback
processing does not block communication, I/O, or DAC servicing.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-10
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-11
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWHVPS-35
TITLE: Manufacturer Hardware Abstraction
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware integrates source code provided by the manufacturer of the microcontroller unit to
interface with various peripherals of the microcontroller. These include functions to interface with
the following peripherals of the microcontroller:

- UART serial port
- GPIO
- SPI serial port
- Flash memory controller
- ADC peripheral
- Timer peripheral
<<<
